WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 263: Andrew Carnegie: The Scottish Bobbin Boy, the Bessemer Converter, and the Gospel of Wealth

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:16.160
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series chronicles the ultimate American dream of industrial steel and boundless philanthropy.

4
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:22.640
[Rebecca] The Scottish Bobbin boy who forged the backbone of a modern nation and gave away his entire fortune.

5
00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:28.400
[Rebecca] Here is George with the life of Andrew Carnegie.

6
00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:39.280
[George] Born in November 1835 in a cramped one-room weaver's stone cottage in Dunfermline, Scotland, Andrew Carnegie entered a world convulsed by the Industrial Revolution.

7
00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:46.960
[George] His father Will was a proud handloom damask weaver whose livelihood was shattered by the introduction of steam-powered textile looms.

8
00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:50.880
[George] His mother Margaret bound shoes to keep starvation from the door.

9
00:00:50.880 --> 00:01:11.440
[George] Imbued with radical Scottish Chartist ideals of political equality, abolitionism, and fierce working-class pride, the impoverished Carnegies borrowed 20 pounds to purchase steerage tickets on the sailing ship Whiskasset, enduring seven miserable weeks on the stormy Atlantic in 1848 to reach Allegheny, Pennsylvania.

10
00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:13.600
[George] Across the river from Pittsburgh.

11
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:28.480
[George] At 12 years old, young Andy began working 12 hours a day, six days a week, in a dark, rat-in-fested cotton factory as a bobbin boy, earning $1.20 a week, moving to a telegraph office as a messenger boy.

12
00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:31.120
[George] Carnegie displayed boundless ambition.

13
00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:44.880
[George] He taught himself to decipher Morse code directly by ear rather than reading paper tape, only the third person in America capable of the feat, earning a reputation for lightning speed and photographic memory that caught the eye of Thomas A.

14
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:49.600
[George] Scott, the dynamic superintendent of the Pennsylvania Railroad.

15
00:01:49.600 --> 00:02:00.920
[George] Young Andrew's Scottish childhood was steeped in the radical Presbyterian covenanting tradition, learning from his maternal grandfather Thomas Morrison to detest hereditary aristocracy and privilege.

16
00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:10.520
[George] His nightly study of European history in Colonel Anderson's library convinced him that knowledge was the true lever of social mobility in a democratic society.

17
00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:23.480
[George] Hired in 1853 as Scott's private secretary and telegraph operator at $35 a month, Carnegie entered the inner command sanctum of the largest and most technologically advanced corporation in the world.

18
00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:36.520
[George] When the Civil War erupted in 1861, Scott was named Assistant Secretary of War, and 25-year-old Carnegie organized military rail transport and telegraph lines for the Union Army.

19
00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:44.760
[George] Personally repairing charred tracks outside Washington and suffering physical burns on his face from dragging fallen telegraph wires.

20
00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:56.280
[George] During his railway years, Carnegie learned the revolutionary principles of modern corporate management, cost accounting, economies of scale, and relentless capital reinvestment.

21
00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:05.240
[George] Guided by Scott, Carnegie made audacious early investments in sleeping cars, locomotive works, and oil wells along Oil Creek.

22
00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:14.520
[George] In 1865, recognizing that the wooden railway bridges across America were constantly catching fire and collapsing under heavy steam locomotives.

23
00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:28.840
[George] Carnegie resigned from the railroad to establish the Keystone Bridge Company, pioneering the manufacture of durable cast iron bridges, culminating in the construction of James Ede's monumental steel arched bridge across the Mississippi River at St.

24
00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:29.560
[George] Louis.

25
00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:40.760
[George] The Keystone Bridge Company's successful spanning of the Allegheny and Ohio Rivers demonstrated the structural superiority of iron trusses over timber frameworks for heavy freight transport.

26
00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:51.280
[George] Carnegie's early partnership with Thomas Scott taught him that controlling transportation corridors was just as vital to industrial dominance as the manufacturing process itself.

27
00:03:51.280 --> 00:04:01.200
[George] In 1872, during a business trip to England, Carnegie visited an English steelworks and witnessed Sir Henry Bessemer's revolutionary pneumatic converter.

28
00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:16.320
[George] A massive pear-shaped vessel blowing blasts of cold air through molten pig iron, burning out carbon in a deafening eruption of sparks and roaring flames, transforming 20 tons of brittle iron into flexible, high-strength steel in 20 minutes.

29
00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:20.800
[George] Carnegie stood transfixed, experiencing an industrial epiphany.

30
00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:22.800
[George] The day of iron is dead.

31
00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:24.880
[George] The day of steel has come.

32
00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:35.920
[George] Rushing back to Pittsburgh, Carnegie organized the Edgar Thompson Steel Works in Braddock, naming it after the president of the Pennsylvania Railroad to secure lucrative rail contracts.

33
00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:38.640
[George] Carnegie mastered vertical integration.

34
00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:51.840
[George] Purchasing rich iron ore ranges in Minnesota's Mesabi Range, thousands of acres of bituminous coal and coke ovens managed by Henry Clay Frick in Connellsville, a private fleet of Great Lakes ore freighters.

35
00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:57.280
[George] And his own rail lines connecting Lake Erie directly to Pittsburgh blast furnaces.

36
00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:10.960
[George] Operating his mills 24 hours a day, Carnegie slashed the price of steel rails from $160 a ton to $12 a ton, fueling the transcontinental rail boom that united the American continent.

37
00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:23.600
[George] The Edgar Thompson Works incorporated the latest chemistry laboratories directly into the blast furnace operations, allowing Carnegie to identify superior iron ores ignored by less rigorous competitors.

38
00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:34.920
[George] Carnegie's relentless focus on reducing unit production costs rather than fixing prices enabled him to weather periodic economic panics while weaker competitors collapsed into bankruptcy.

39
00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:43.640
[George] The darkest stain upon Carnegie's reputation occurred in July 1892 at the massive homestead steel works along the Monongahela River.

40
00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:56.920
[George] While Carnegie publicly proclaimed himself a champion of trade unions and workingmen's rights, he privately instructed his hardline chairman Henry Clay Frick to break the amalgamated association of iron and steel workers.

41
00:05:56.920 --> 00:06:03.720
[George] Carnegie departed for his isolated summer estate in the Scottish Highlands, leaving Frick in absolute command.

42
00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:15.800
[George] Frick locked out 3,800 workers, constructed a three-mile timber wall topped with barbed wire nicknamed Fort Frick, and hired 300 armed Pinkerton detectives to retake the mill.

43
00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:31.000
[George] In the early morning hours of July 6, 1892, as the Pinkertons were towed upriver on barges, thousands of locked-out workers and townspeople engaged in a pitched, day-long battle with rifles, dynamite, and a brass cannon.

44
00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:34.120
[George] Nine strikers and seven Pinkertons were killed.

45
00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:43.160
[George] The state militia occupied Homestead, the union was crushed, and non union wages were slashed by 30% for 84-hour workweeks.

46
00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:49.560
[George] Though Carnegie claimed he was absent and powerless, the bloodshed haunted his conscience for the rest of his days.

47
00:06:49.560 --> 00:07:01.480
[George] The bloody clash on the riverbanks at Homestead shattered Carnegie's romantic vision of peaceful labor relations, exposing the irreconcilable tension between corporate capital and industrial labor.

48
00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:12.120
[George] The brutal aftermath of the strike left deep generational scars across Pittsburgh, transforming the one-sprowed steel town into an authoritarian non-union company enclave.

49
00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:22.160
[George] By 1900, the Carnegie Steel Company was producing more steel than the entire British Empire, generating $40 million in net annual profit.

50
00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:29.520
[George] Carnegie, now 65 years old, wished to retire to Scotland and devote his remaining years to philanthropy.

51
00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:37.200
[George] In December 1900, at a grand banquet at the University Club in New York, Carnegie's brilliant young protege Charles M.

52
00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:42.480
[George] Schwab delivered an electrifying speech on the future of an integrated global steel trust.

53
00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:45.200
[George] In the audience sat Wall Street Colossus J.

54
00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:46.720
[George] Pierpont Morgan.

55
00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:51.680
[George] Fascinated, Morgan asked Schwab to discover Carnegie's asking price.

56
00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:56.080
[George] Schwab visited Carnegie at his Manhattan mansion on 91st Street.

57
00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:01.520
[George] After a round of golf, Carnegie jotted down a number in pencil on a scrap of paper.

58
00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:07.040
[George] $480 million, equivalent to nearly $16 billion today.

59
00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:11.200
[George] Schwab brought the slip of paper to Morgan at 23 Wall Street.

60
00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:16.720
[George] Morgan glanced at the figure, paused for three seconds, and replied, I accept.

61
00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:22.960
[George] Days later, Morgan visited Carnegie to conclude the purchase, shaking his hand and saying, Mr.

62
00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:27.680
[George] Carnegie, I want to congratulate you on being the richest man in the world.

63
00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:36.320
[George] Morgan merged Carnegie Steel with rival companies to create the United States Steel Corporation, the world's first billion-dollar corporation.

64
00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:39.760
[George] The negotiations between Charles Schwab and J.P.

65
00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:49.200
[George] Morgan at the University Club marked the climactic transition of American capitalism from competitive owner managers to massive investment banking trusts.

66
00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:59.920
[George] Carnegie's pencil-written price of $480 million was paid entirely in gold-backed bonds, securing his family's absolute financial independence for generations.

67
00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:16.440
[George] In 1889, Carnegie had published his revolutionary philosophical essay, The Gospel of Wealth, asserting that surplus wealth was a sacred trust that rich men were morally obligated to distribute during their lifetimes for the permanent elevation of the human race.

68
00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:22.040
[George] Carnegie declared with burning conviction the man who dies rich dies disgraced.

69
00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:29.080
[George] For the next 18 years, Carnegie embarked on a philanthropic campaign of staggering, unprecedented magnitude.

70
00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:40.680
[George] Remembering the small private library of Colonel James Anderson in Allegheny that had opened its doors to working boys on Saturday afternoons, Carnegie poured his fortune into public education.

71
00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:51.560
[George] Funding the construction of 2,509 public libraries across the English-speaking world, built of solid stone with open bookshelves accessible to every citizen.

72
00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:54.200
[George] He built Carnegie Hall in New York.

73
00:09:54.200 --> 00:10:14.840
[George] Established the Carnegie Institute of Technology in Pittsburgh, created the Carnegie Institution for Science in Washington, founded the Carnegie Foundation for the Advancement of Teaching, which established the modern university pension system, and created the Carnegie Hero Fund to reward everyday citizens who risked their lives to save others.

74
00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:26.120
[George] Carnegie's library philanthropy required municipal governments to donate building sites and levy a permanent annual tax of 10% to purchase books and pay librarian salaries.

75
00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:37.640
[George] The Carnegie Hero Fund Commission distributed bronze, silver, and gold medals alongside financial pensions to widows of ordinary citizens who gave their lives to rescue strangers.

76
00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:41.240
[George] Carnegie's ultimate passion was the abolition of war.

77
00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:56.240
[George] Believing that arbitration treaties could replace military slaughter, Carnegie poured 10 million dollars into the Carnegie Endowment for International Peace and constructed the magnificent Peace Palace at The Hague in the Netherlands to house the permanent court of arbitration.

78
00:10:56.240 --> 00:11:07.600
[George] But in August 1914, the catastrophic outbreak of World War I shattered his optimistic Victorian faith in human progress, breaking the elderly philanthropist's heart.

79
00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:17.520
[George] Retiring to his estate Shadowbrook in Lennox, Massachusetts, Andrew Carnegie died peacefully of bronchial pneumonia in August 1919 at age 83.

80
00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:26.720
[George] By the time of his death, he had given away over $350 million, retaining only a small fraction of his estate for his wife and daughter.

81
00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:30.560
[George] Andrew Carnegie's life was a study in immense contradiction.

82
00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:44.640
[George] The ruthless Ironmaster whose mills broke unions and squeezed laborers, and the visionary humanitarian whose stone libraries opened the doors of literature, science, and dignity to millions of working-class families across the globe.

83
00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:55.520
[George] His dream of universal peace through international arbitration reflected his enduring Victorian faith that human reason and moral enlightenment could triumph over primitive militarism.

84
00:11:55.520 --> 00:12:07.920
[George] Carnegie's legacy remains visible in the stone libraries that still anchor small towns across America and Britain, living monuments to the Bobbin boy who believed that education was the highest gift of wealth.

85
00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:27.600
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

